You are grounding a direct weight-edit task. Before any weight of the target model is touched, establish what the model actually does under the current harness — measured, not assumed — and which row of the vendor card the run is meant to follow.

## Doctrine

Measure before you diagnose. In the anchor case, a Qwen reasoning model reported as "bad at math" was finishing almost no math answers at all: 85 of its first 96 practice answers ran to the token limit. The "math bug" was a completion bug. The harness's class counts — correct-and-complete, wrong-but-complete, hit-limit — are the ground every later step stands on.

The vendor model card is part of the ground. A card can recommend several sampling rows (the Qwen3.5-0.8B card has four: thinking or non-thinking, text or vision-language). The anchor ran its target in thinking mode — the condition the card warns is prone to repetitive loops — at settings that match none of the card's rows. A bug that disappears at a card row's settings is a settings bug, not a weight bug.

